Sure, here are more detailed instructions on setting up routes and integrations for the WebSocket API in AWS API Gateway:

1. Set up Routes

Routes define how the incoming requests to your WebSocket API are handled. Follow the steps below to set up routes:

- Go to your API Gateway console and select your WebSocket API. - Click on "Routes" in the left navigation panel and then click on "Create Route". - Choose a route key for your route, such as $connect or $default. - You can also specify the Authorization type required for the route. - Once you have set up the route, you can add integrations to it. 2. Set up Integrations

Integrations define how WebSocket messages are processed by the backend service. Follow the steps below to set up integrations:

2.1 Set up a Lambda function integration:

- Go to your API Gateway console and select your WebSocket API. - Click on "Routes" in the left navigation panel and select the route that you would like to add an integration to. - Click on "Add Integration" and select "Lambda Function". - Choose a Lambda function from the drop-down menu and select the appropriate Lambda Function version and alias for this integration. - Click on "Save" to create the integration. 2.2 Set up an HTTP Integration:

- Go to your API Gateway console and select your WebSocket API. - Click on "Routes" in the left navigation panel and select the route that you would like to add an integration to. - Click on "Add Integration" and select "HTTP API". - Enter the endpoint URL for your backend service and select the HTTP method you want to use. - You may also need to add additional headers, parameters, or request/response mapping templates depending on your backend service and message formats. - Click on "Save" to create the integration. 3. Testing the API with WebSocket Clients

To test the WebSocket API, you can use WebSocket clients like wscat or the Chrome DevTools WebSocket console. You can connect to your WebSocket API using the URL and endpoint, and then test your routes and integrations. Here is an example of how to set up a Lambda function integration for a WebSocket API route:

```javascript
const AWS = require('aws-sdk');
exports.handler = async function(event) {
  console.log("Event received: ", JSON.stringify(event));

  // Handle connect route
  if (event.requestContext.routeKey === "$connect") {
    return {
      statusCode: 200,
      body: "Connected successfully." };
  }

  // Handle default route
  if (event.requestContext.routeKey === "$default") {
    const message = event.body;
    console.log("Message received: ", message);

    // Process message and return response
    return {
      statusCode: 200,
      body: "Response message"
    };
  }
}
```

In this example, the Lambda function handles the $connect and $default routes. For the $connect route, it returns a successful connection message. For the $default route, it processes the incoming message and returns a response.

