First, we create a Node class to represent a single node in the linked list:

```csharp
public class Node
{
    public object Data { get; set; }
    public Node Next { get; set; }

    public Node(object data)
    {
        Data = data;
        Next = null;
    }
}
```

The Node class has two properties: Data, which stores the data for this node, and Next, which stores a reference to the next node in the linked list. We also have a constructor that takes the initial data for the node.

Next, we create the LinkedList class to hold the nodes and provide methods for adding, removing, and searching for nodes:

```csharp
public class LinkedList
{
    private Node head;
    private Node tail;

    public int Count { get; private set; }

    public LinkedList()
    {
        head = null;
        tail = null;
        Count = 0;
    }

    public void AddToHead(object data)
    {
        Node newNode = new Node(data);

        if (head == null)
        {
            head = newNode;
            tail = newNode;
        }
        else
        {
            newNode.Next = head;
            head = newNode;
        }

        Count++;
    }

    public void AddToTail(object data)
    {
        Node newNode = new Node(data);

        if (tail == null)
        {
            head = newNode;
            tail = newNode;
        }
        else
        {
            tail.Next = newNode;
            tail = newNode;
        }

        Count++;
    }

    public object RemoveFromHead()
    {
        if (head == null)
        {
            throw new InvalidOperationException("Cannot remove from an empty list");
        }

        object data = head.Data;

        if (head == tail)
        {
            head = null;
            tail = null;
        }
        else
        {
            head = head.Next;
        }

        Count--;
        return data;
    }

    public object RemoveFromTail()
    {
        if (tail == null)
        {
            throw new InvalidOperationException("Cannot remove from an empty list");
        }

        object data = tail.Data;

        if (head == tail)
        {
            head = null;
            tail = null;
        }
        else
        {
            Node current = head;
            while (current.Next != tail)
            {
                current = current.Next;
            }

            tail = current;
            tail.Next = null;
        }

        Count--;
        return data;
    }

    public Node Search(object data)
    {
        Node current = head;

        while (current != null)
        {
            if (current.Data.Equals(data))
            {
                return current;
            }

            current = current.Next;
        }

        return null;
    }
}
```

The LinkedList class has two private fields: head and tail, which store references to the first and last nodes in the list, respectively. We also have a public Count property to keep track of how many nodes are in the list.

The class has a constructor that initializes the head, tail, and Count fields to null and 0, respectively.

The AddToHead method creates a new node with the specified data, and adds it to the head of the list. If the list is empty, the new node becomes both the head and tail of the list. If the list is not empty, the new node is inserted at the beginning of the list, and its next property is set to the previous head.

The AddToTail method works similarly to AddToHead, but instead adds the new node to the end of the list.

The RemoveFromHead method removes the node at the head of the list and returns its data. If the list is empty, an InvalidOperationException is thrown. If the list has only one node, both the head and tail are set to null. If the list has more than one node, the head is set to the next node in the list.

The RemoveFromTail method removes the node at the tail of the list and returns its data. This method is a bit more complex than RemoveFromHead, because we need to traverse the list to find the second-to-last node in order to update its next property to null.

The Search method takes a piece of data and searches the list for a node that contains that data. If found, the method returns the node; if not found, it returns null.

Here's an example of how to use the LinkedList class:

```csharp
LinkedList list = new LinkedList();
list.AddToHead("foo");
list.AddToTail("bar");

Console.WriteLine(list.Count); // output: 2

object headData = list.RemoveFromHead();
Console.WriteLine(headData); // output: foo

Node node = list.Search("bar");
Console.WriteLine(node.Data); // output: bar
```

