```csharp
public void InsertNodeAtIndex(object data, int index)
{
    if (index < 0 || index > Count)
    {
        throw new ArgumentOutOfRangeException(nameof(index), "Index is out of range");
    }

    Node newNode = new Node(data);

    if (index == 0)
    {
        newNode.Next = head;
        head = newNode;
        if (tail == null)
        {
            tail = newNode;
        }
    }
    else if (index == Count)
    {
        tail.Next = newNode;
        tail = newNode;
    }
    else
    {
        Node current = head;
        for (int I = 0; I < index - 1; i++)
        {
            current = current.Next;
        }
        newNode.Next = current.Next;
        current.Next = newNode;
    }

    Count++;
}
```

This method takes two parameters: the data for the new node, and the index at which to insert the node. It first checks that the index is within the bounds of the list.

If the index is 0, we insert the new node at the head of the list. We set the new node's Next property to the current head, then update head to point to the new node. If the list is empty, we also update tail to point to the new node.

If the index is equal to the list's Count (meaning the node should be inserted at the end of the list), we simply update tail to point to the new node, and update the new node's Next property to null.

If the index is somewhere in the middle of the list, we traverse the list to find the node immediately before the desired index (i.e. The node before which we want to insert the new node). Once we find that node, we set the new node's Next property to its current Next (i.e. The node that was originally at the desired index), then update the previous node's Next property to the new node.

We increment the Count property after successfully inserting the new node.

Here's an example of how to use InsertNodeAtIndex:

```csharp
LinkedList list = new LinkedList();
list.AddToHead("foo");
list.AddToTail("bar");
list.InsertNodeAtIndex("baz", 1);

Console.WriteLine(list.Count); // output: 3

object data = list.RemoveFromTail();
Console.WriteLine(data); // output: bar

Node node = list.Search("baz");
Console.WriteLine(node.Data); // output: baz
```

In this example, we create a new LinkedList, add two nodes to it (one to the head and one to the tail), insert a new node with data "baz" at index 1, print the Count, remove the node from the tail, and search for the node with data "baz".

