To create an initial stage where the user types in the name of their ranch and then starts a new world using Unity Cloud Save, follow these steps:

1. Set up Unity Cloud Save: Make sure you have Unity Cloud Save enabled for your project. To enable it, sign up for a Unity account or log in to your existing Unity account. Go to the Unity Dashboard (https://dashboard.unity.com), choose your project, and enable Unity Cloud Save in the Services tab.

2. Create a UI for the initial stage: In your Unity project, create a new scene for the initial stage. In this scene, create UI elements such as a Text component for the prompt, an InputField for the user to type in the ranch name, and a Button to confirm the input and proceed to the next scene (your game world). Make sure to assign a Canvas to hold the UI elements, and adjust the components to suit your desired layout and design.

3. Create a script to handle user input: Create a new C# script in your Unity project and name it something like "InitialStageManager". Attach this script to an empty GameObject in the initial stage scene. In this script, you will handle user input and manage the transition to the new world scene.

4. Save the ranch name using Unity Cloud Save: In your "InitialStageManager" script, import the necessary namespaces for Unity Cloud Save at the top:

```csharp
using UnityEngine.Cloud;
using UnityEngine.Cloud.Analytics;
```

5. Add reference to the UI elements in the "InitialStageManager" script:

```csharp
public InputField ranchNameInput;
public Button startButton;
```

After that, connect the InputField and Button in the Unity Editor by dragging the corresponding UI elements from the scene hierarchy to the relevant script fields in the Inspector.

6. Add a method to confirm input and transition to the new world, and register the button click event:

```csharp
private void Start()
{
    startButton.onClick.AddListener(HandleStartButtonClick);
}

private void HandleStartButtonClick()
{
    // Save the ranch name using Unity Cloud Save
    string ranchName = ranchNameInput.text;
    UnityAnalytics.SetCustomUserID(ranchName);

    // TODO: Save any other relevant data for the new world

    // Load the new world scene
    SceneManager.LoadScene("YourNewWorldSceneName");
}
```

Don't forget to import the SceneManager namespace at the top of the script:

```csharp
using UnityEngine.SceneManagement;
```

7. Finally, build your game for your target mobile platform and test it. The initial stage should now allow users to type in their ranch name and proceed to the new world. The ranch name will be saved using Unity Cloud Save, making it accessible across devices.

Note that in this example, I've used `UnityAnalytics.SetCustomUserID()` to save the ranch name—this is only a basic way of saving the data. You may want to explore using more advanced cloud saving mechanisms provided by Unity Cloud Save for further customization and robustness.

To implement an unlock barn function using Unity Cloud Save, you can follow these steps:

1. Set up PlayerPrefs to store the in-game currency and unlocked barns' status:

In your existing game management script (or create a new script if you don't have one), create methods to save and load the user's in-game currency and the unlocked barns' statuses. You can use PlayerPrefs for this purpose since it works well with Unity Cloud Save.

```csharp
private const string CURRENCY_KEY = "user_currency";
private const string BARN_STATUS_KEY_PREFIX = "barn_status_";

private int currentCurrency;

public void SaveCurrency(int currencyAmount)
{
    PlayerPrefs.SetInt(CURRENCY_KEY, currencyAmount);
    PlayerPrefs.Save();
    currentCurrency = currencyAmount;
}

public int LoadCurrency()
{
    currentCurrency = PlayerPrefs.GetInt(CURRENCY_KEY, 0);
    return currentCurrency;
}

public void SaveBarnStatus(int barnID, bool isUnlocked)
{
    PlayerPrefs.SetInt(BARN_STATUS_KEY_PREFIX + barnID.ToString(), isUnlocked ? 1 : 0);
    PlayerPrefs.Save();
}

public bool LoadBarnStatus(int barnID)
{
    return PlayerPrefs.GetInt(BARN_STATUS_KEY_PREFIX + barnID.ToString(), 0) == 1; // 1 = unlocked, 0 = locked
}
```

2. Create an "UnlockBarn" method in the game management script:

```csharp
public bool UnlockBarn(int barnID, int cost)
{
    if(currentCurrency < cost)
    {
        return false; // Not enough currency to unlock the barn.
