The CSS property `.box:hover` is related to the hover pseudo-class in CSS. It means when the mouse pointer is over the element with the class value 'box', the associated styles will be applied.

In other words, it denotes a change in presentation of the element of class 'box' when the user 'hovers' over this element. The properties defined within `.box:hover` will change from the default properties to the defined ones when a cursor is positioned over the corresponding element.

A typical application could be:

```css
.box {
  width: 200px;
  height: 200px;
  background-color: blue;
}

.box:hover {
  background-color: red;
}
```

In this example, a box of 200px square would initially display a blue background. When a user hovers over the box with their cursor, the background color would change to red. When the user moves their cursor away from the box, the box's background color would revert to blue.

