- We initialize the diagonal elements (i.e., substrings of length 1) to 1, since each character is a palindrome by itself.
- We then iterate over all possible substring lengths (`len`) greater than 1, calculating the length of the LPS for each substring of length `len`.
- For each possible substring, we again iterate over all possible starting indices of the substrings, i.e., over `i` from 0 to `n-len`. We then calculate the ending index `j` of the substring. 
- If the first and last characters of the substring are the same, we add 2 to the length of the LPS of the substring excluding the first and last characters. If they are different, we take the maximum of the LPS length of the substring excluding the last character and of the substring excluding the first character. 
- Finally, we print the length of the longest palindromic subsequence and the subsequence itself by passing the input string `str`, the `dp` array and the starting and ending indices to the `printLPS` function.

