Calculating the sum of digits in a floating-point number requires a different approach. One way to do it is to convert the floating-point number to a string, remove the decimal point and then sum the digits as before. Here's an example program that demonstrates this:

```
import java.util.Scanner;

public class SumOfDigitsFloat {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);
        System.out.print("Enter a floating-point number: ");
        double num = scanner.nextDouble();

        int sum = 0;
        // convert the number to a string
        String numStr = Double.toString(num);
        // remove the decimal point from the string
        numStr = numStr.replace(". ", "");

        // sum the digits in the string
        for (int I = 0; I < numStr.length(); i++) {
            char digitChar = numStr.charAt(i);
            int digit = Character.getNumericValue(digitChar);
            sum += digit;
        }

        System.out.println("The sum of the digits is: " + sum);
    }
}
```

In this program, we first prompt the user to enter a floating-point number using a Scanner object. We then initialize a variable to hold the sum of the digits to 0. We then convert the floating-point number to a string using Double.toString() and remove the decimal point from the string using the replace() method. This leaves us with just the digits of the number as a string. We then loop through each character in the string and use the Character.getNumericValue() method to convert the character to an integer, which is added to the sum variable. This process is repeated until we have gone through each digit in the number. Finally, we print out the sum of the digits using System.out.println(). To test the program, we can enter different input values and verify that the output is correct. For example:

